WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:10.760
In the Taiwan-Korea semiconductor battle

00:00:10.760 --> 00:00:12.429
who will be the last one standing?

00:00:12.429 --> 00:00:13.763
Since the founding of TSMC

00:00:13.763 --> 00:00:15.432
its greatest competitor has always been

00:00:15.432 --> 00:00:17.684
another semiconductor giant—

00:00:17.684 --> 00:00:18.518
Samsung from South Korea

00:00:18.518 --> 00:00:19.686
In 1984

00:00:19.686 --> 00:00:22.814
Samsung began producing 64Kb DRAM for Intel

00:00:22.814 --> 00:00:23.815
By 1987

00:00:23.815 --> 00:00:25.525
Samsung had already developed

00:00:25.525 --> 00:00:26.943
cutting-edge, high-quality

00:00:26.943 --> 00:00:28.194
4Mb DRAM--

00:00:28.194 --> 00:00:31.031
the same year that TSMC was founded

00:00:31.031 --> 00:00:33.616
The first generation Apple iPhone chips

00:00:33.616 --> 00:00:35.744
were exclusively manufactured by Samsung

00:00:35.785 --> 00:00:38.955
Even though they were fierce rivals in the smartphone market

00:00:38.955 --> 00:00:42.042
TSMC and Samsung waged an all-out war after the millennium

00:00:42.083 --> 00:00:44.419
How did TSMC break the deadlock?

00:00:44.419 --> 00:00:45.462
Has TSMC really won

00:00:45.462 --> 00:00:47.422
a decisive victory?

00:02:32.068 --> 00:02:33.319
We mentioned in the last episode

00:02:33.319 --> 00:02:35.905
that Sun Yun-suan, Li Kwoh-ting, and Morris Chang

00:02:35.905 --> 00:02:37.866
worked together to promote the development of TSMC

00:02:37.866 --> 00:02:39.325
embedding Taiwan

00:02:39.325 --> 00:02:41.578
at the core of the global technology industry

00:02:41.578 --> 00:02:42.620
We must say

00:02:42.620 --> 00:02:44.914
Sun Yun-suan and Li Kwoh-ting had a keen eye for talent

00:02:44.956 --> 00:02:46.124
They found the optimal position for Taiwan

00:02:46.124 --> 00:02:48.001
from both economic and political perspectives

00:02:48.001 --> 00:02:50.128
in the 1960s

00:02:50.170 --> 00:02:51.129
Politically

00:02:51.129 --> 00:02:53.840
from 1960 to the early 1970s

00:02:53.840 --> 00:02:56.217
the U.S. was deeply entangled in the Vietnam war

00:02:56.259 --> 00:02:57.385
in 1964

00:02:57.385 --> 00:02:59.053
China conducted its first nuclear test.

00:02:59.053 --> 00:03:00.847
President Chiang Kai-Shek of Taiwan at that time

00:03:00.847 --> 00:03:02.807
was contemplating the Project Guoguang

00:03:02.807 --> 00:03:04.475
secretly aiming to reclaim the Chinese mainland

00:03:04.475 --> 00:03:06.769
Asia was far from stabe

00:03:06.769 --> 00:03:08.021
The U.S. desperately needed an ally

00:03:08.021 --> 00:03:10.440
for long-term collaboration in Southeast Asia.

00:03:10.440 --> 00:03:13.401
The sisal hemp factory operated by veterans in Gangshan

00:03:13.401 --> 00:03:15.737
was one of Taiwan’s emerging industries

00:03:15.737 --> 00:03:18.239
It utilized Taiwan’s abundant sisal hemp supply

00:03:18.239 --> 00:03:20.033
braiding it into ropes

00:03:20.033 --> 00:03:22.785
for both domestic and international ships.

00:03:22.785 --> 00:03:23.745
Economically

00:03:23.786 --> 00:03:24.996
in the 1960s

00:03:24.996 --> 00:03:26.247
Taiwan was actively promoting

00:03:26.247 --> 00:03:28.708
labor-intensive export industries

00:03:28.708 --> 00:03:31.044
The import substitution policy of the previous decade

00:03:31.085 --> 00:03:33.838
transformed Taiwan from an agricultural economy to an industrial one

00:03:33.838 --> 00:03:35.256
resulting in massive productive capacity

00:03:35.256 --> 00:03:37.675
which at times seemed excessive for the domestic market.

00:03:37.675 --> 00:03:38.259
So

00:03:38.259 --> 00:03:40.678
in the 1960s, the policy shifted toward export orientation 

00:03:40.678 --> 00:03:42.555
attracting investments from advanced industrial countries

00:03:42.555 --> 00:03:45.600
with its abundant supply of cheap labor

00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:48.603
to help mitigate domestic issues caused by soaring wages

00:03:48.603 --> 00:03:50.605
The governmentset up many Export Processing Zones

00:03:50.605 --> 00:03:54.400
for light industries like appliances, textiles &amp; plastics

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:54.943
The timing was perfect—

00:03:54.943 --> 00:03:57.237
if Morris Chang of TI could be recruited

00:03:57.237 --> 00:03:59.072
to launch semiconductor industry in Taiwan

00:03:59.072 --> 00:04:01.532
The right time, place and people would be aligned

00:04:01.532 --> 00:04:04.285
As Korea, Singapore, and Malaysia

00:04:04.327 --> 00:04:06.246
entered the semiconductor competition one after another

00:04:06.246 --> 00:04:08.331
Taiwan was ready to join as well

00:04:08.331 --> 00:04:11.334
But the challenge for TSMC has just begun

00:04:11.334 --> 00:04:13.044
TSMC was founded in 1987

00:04:13.044 --> 00:04:16.923
and went public on the Taiwan Stock Exchange in 1994

00:04:16.923 --> 00:04:18.424
As operations stabilized

00:04:18.424 --> 00:04:19.926
the next challenge was

00:04:19.926 --> 00:04:22.262
competing with other manufacturers

00:04:22.262 --> 00:04:24.222
such as Samsung and Intel

00:04:24.222 --> 00:04:25.306
In this market

00:04:25.306 --> 00:04:27.100
competition was not only about production capacity

00:04:27.100 --> 00:04:29.519
but also about who could make smaller transistors

00:04:29.519 --> 00:04:31.771
and pack them into a chip

00:04:31.771 --> 00:04:33.898
Yet as transistors shrank

00:04:33.898 --> 00:04:35.775
new problemsemerged

00:04:35.775 --> 00:04:36.901
Let’s review

00:04:36.943 --> 00:04:39.404
A transistor functions like a small water valve

00:04:39.404 --> 00:04:41.322
thatforms complex logic circuits

00:04:41.322 --> 00:04:42.949
through a series of on-and-off switches.

00:04:42.949 --> 00:04:45.368
The small water valve in a transistor

00:04:45.368 --> 00:04:47.704
is called a “gate”, similar to a water gate

00:04:47.704 --> 00:04:49.414
The inlet and outlet pipes

00:04:49.414 --> 00:04:51.624
are called the “source” and “drain”

00:04:51.624 --> 00:04:53.126
The source and drain are like

00:04:53.126 --> 00:04:56.296
two reservoirs at the upstream and downstream ends of a river

00:04:56.296 --> 00:04:58.798
To move water from the source to the drain

00:04:58.798 --> 00:05:02.051
the water valve controlled by the gate must be opened

00:05:02.051 --> 00:05:02.593
Among them

00:05:02.593 --> 00:05:05.596
in the most popular transistor structure used in logic chips

00:05:05.596 --> 00:05:06.848
the gate has a sandwich-like structure

00:05:06.848 --> 00:05:08.933
composed of metal, an insulating oxide layer

00:05:08.933 --> 00:05:11.644
and semiconductor material

00:05:11.686 --> 00:05:13.187
Transistors of this type

00:05:13.187 --> 00:05:14.939
are commonly known as MOSFETs

00:05:15.690 --> 00:05:18.151
A single transistor structure

00:05:18.151 --> 00:05:20.695
But TSMC can now fit 200 billion tiny transistors like this

00:05:20.695 --> 00:05:23.823
in a single chip

00:05:23.823 --> 00:05:26.534
This number is expected to surpass a trillion soon

00:05:26.534 --> 00:05:27.493
How?

00:05:27.493 --> 00:05:28.161
Obviously

00:05:28.161 --> 00:05:31.164
the only way is to further shrink transistors

00:05:31.164 --> 00:05:32.999
But as transistors shrink

00:05:32.999 --> 00:05:34.834
the first challenge they face is

00:05:34.834 --> 00:05:36.419
water valve leakage

00:05:36.461 --> 00:05:37.587
Usually

00:05:37.587 --> 00:05:38.671
the water valves in transistors

00:05:38.671 --> 00:05:40.673
are designed to shut tightly

00:05:40.673 --> 00:05:42.300
to resist horizontal hydraulic pressure

00:05:42.300 --> 00:05:45.011
namely the vertical electric field applied to the gate

00:05:45.053 --> 00:05:49.182
must be far stronger than horizontal field between the source and drain

00:05:49.182 --> 00:05:50.725
If a closed water valve

00:05:50.725 --> 00:05:52.643
fails to completely stop current flow

00:05:52.643 --> 00:05:54.145
water spills over

00:05:54.145 --> 00:05:56.022
leading to increased power consumption

00:05:56.022 --> 00:05:58.066
Your smartphone would always need  to be connected to a power bank

00:05:58.066 --> 00:06:01.069
or worse, the water valve could fail entirely

00:06:01.069 --> 00:06:02.028
causing a total breakdown

00:06:02.028 --> 00:06:03.571
The entire transistor becomes invalid

00:06:03.571 --> 00:06:05.198
As transistors shrink

00:06:05.198 --> 00:06:07.200
the driving voltage used in circuits

00:06:07.200 --> 00:06:09.702
remained at about 0.7 volts

00:06:09.744 --> 00:06:11.287
It means the valve is getting smaller

00:06:11.287 --> 00:06:12.955
while the hydraulic pressure stays the same

00:06:12.955 --> 00:06:13.873
The valve ends up

00:06:13.873 --> 00:06:15.166
unable to stop leakage

00:06:15.166 --> 00:06:17.377
caused by overflow current

00:06:17.710 --> 00:06:18.711
And it’s not just leakage--

00:06:18.711 --> 00:06:21.422
When the source and drain get closer

00:06:21.422 --> 00:06:22.924
various short-channel effects happen

00:06:22.924 --> 00:06:24.926
just like elderly folks

00:06:24.926 --> 00:06:26.594
develop more chronic diseases over time

00:06:26.594 --> 00:06:28.012
including velocity saturation

00:06:28.012 --> 00:06:29.180
parasitic BJT

00:06:29.180 --> 00:06:30.348
hot carrier effects

00:06:30.348 --> 00:06:32.100
and threshold voltage roll-off, among others.

00:06:32.100 --> 00:06:35.395
Engineers struggled to keep up

00:06:35.395 --> 00:06:36.979
just like doctors watching red figures on a health report

00:06:37.855 --> 00:06:39.732
For the miniaturization limit of transistors

00:06:39.732 --> 00:06:41.609
have we found a solution?

00:06:41.609 --> 00:06:44.362
To address the chronic disease called short channel effects

00:06:44.404 --> 00:06:46.948
academia and industry have worked together

00:06:46.948 --> 00:06:49.575
proposing various methods to keep Moore’s Law going

00:06:49.575 --> 00:06:52.078
For example, semiconductor materials are modified

00:06:52.078 --> 00:06:53.996
by doping silicon with other elements

00:06:53.996 --> 00:06:56.249
To suppress leakage precisely

00:06:56.249 --> 00:06:58.960
materials experts experimented with mixing different elements

00:06:58.960 --> 00:07:00.503
at specific locations within transistors

00:07:00.503 --> 00:07:01.921
much like knitting a wool blanket

00:07:01.921 --> 00:07:03.464
placing fibers of different colors

00:07:03.464 --> 00:07:05.258
in designated areas

00:07:05.258 --> 00:07:07.969
The technique is called ion implantation

00:07:07.969 --> 00:07:09.178
Who would have thought that semiconductors

00:07:09.178 --> 00:07:10.471
could draw inspiration from handicraft?

00:07:11.431 --> 00:07:12.598
Another approach was

00:07:12.598 --> 00:07:15.601
to thin the insulation oxide layer in transistors

00:07:15.643 --> 00:07:18.438
making it easier for the vertical electric field to pass through the gate channel

00:07:18.438 --> 00:07:20.231
thereby enhancing control over the water valve

00:07:20.773 --> 00:07:21.232
However

00:07:21.232 --> 00:07:23.818
when the insulation layer becomes thinner than 5 nanometers

00:07:23.818 --> 00:07:26.571
gate leakage starts

00:07:26.571 --> 00:07:28.281
due to quantum tunneling effects

00:07:28.281 --> 00:07:30.199
Though this solved the horizontal leakage issue

00:07:30.199 --> 00:07:32.285
the valve that was supposed to block current flow

00:07:32.285 --> 00:07:33.077
wow

00:07:33.077 --> 00:07:35.204
now starts leaking itself

00:07:35.204 --> 00:07:36.581
To solve this problem

00:07:36.581 --> 00:07:38.958
Intel chose to change the insulationmaterial

00:07:38.958 --> 00:07:41.169
at the node of 45 nanometers process

00:07:41.169 --> 00:07:42.295
from silicon dioxide

00:07:42.295 --> 00:07:45.256
to hafnium oxide which has a higher dielectric constant

00:07:45.256 --> 00:07:47.967
This significantly reduced gate leakage

00:07:47.967 --> 00:07:50.470
The tech was quickly adopted by semiconductor companies

00:07:50.511 --> 00:07:53.222
and remains a key innovation till now

00:07:53.222 --> 00:07:55.516
Even with such efforts

00:07:55.516 --> 00:07:57.518
as transistors keep shrinking

00:07:57.560 --> 00:07:59.687
to the node about 20 nanometers

00:07:59.687 --> 00:08:02.106
these solutions began to wane

00:08:02.106 --> 00:08:03.399
And too much mixing

00:08:03.399 --> 00:08:05.443
not only made the process more complicated

00:08:05.443 --> 00:08:07.570
but also lowered unit efficacy

00:08:07.570 --> 00:08:09.363
Ultimately the breakthrough came

00:08:09.363 --> 00:08:12.033
with an entirely new transistor structure

00:08:12.033 --> 00:08:13.659
the Fin Field-Effect Transistor

00:08:13.659 --> 00:08:14.660
or FinFET for short

00:08:18.164 --> 00:08:19.790
The original structure of transistors

00:08:19.790 --> 00:08:22.585
can be visualized as a 2D flat

00:08:22.585 --> 00:08:23.920
The source and drain

00:08:23.961 --> 00:08:27.006
were semiconductor materials embedded on a flat substrate

00:08:27.006 --> 00:08:29.800
The gate applied voltage from above

00:08:29.842 --> 00:08:30.885
meaning

00:08:30.885 --> 00:08:33.888
the gate could onlyshut vertically downwards

00:08:33.888 --> 00:08:35.681
As channels became shorter

00:08:35.681 --> 00:08:37.600
the gate could no longer shut tightly

00:08:37.600 --> 00:08:40.853
That’s when Masaru Hisamoto from Hitachi central research lab

00:08:40.853 --> 00:08:42.188
had a groundbreaking idea

00:08:42.188 --> 00:08:44.565
If we change the shape and structure of the electrodes

00:08:44.565 --> 00:08:48.569
the gate could be shut from three directions simultaneously

00:08:48.569 --> 00:08:50.738
This means transforming planar electrodes

00:08:50.738 --> 00:08:53.950
into vertical fin-like structures

00:08:53.950 --> 00:08:57.745
with the gate straddling the standing channel

00:08:57.787 --> 00:08:58.871
As a result

00:08:58.871 --> 00:09:01.707
the gate can touch the channel

00:09:01.707 --> 00:09:02.959
from above and on both sides

00:09:03.000 --> 00:09:04.961
forming a 3-sided gate

00:09:04.961 --> 00:09:07.004
When we need to shut the gate

00:09:07.004 --> 00:09:09.423
the gate’s electric field can block the channel effectively

00:09:09.423 --> 00:09:11.509
from 3 directions

00:09:11.509 --> 00:09:12.885
The gate in this MOSFET structure

00:09:12.885 --> 00:09:14.136
is still metal

00:09:14.136 --> 00:09:15.304
Insulating oxide layer

00:09:15.304 --> 00:09:17.557
and semiconductor 

00:09:17.557 --> 00:09:19.809
The metal gate and thin oxide layer

00:09:19.809 --> 00:09:22.228
clamp the fin-shaped channel

00:09:22.228 --> 00:09:23.729
which connects the source and drain

00:09:23.729 --> 00:09:26.732
The fin-shaped 3D design

00:09:26.732 --> 00:09:30.403
was later commonly referred as FinFET

00:09:30.403 --> 00:09:31.195
At that time

00:09:31.195 --> 00:09:32.405
Masaru Hisamoto was conducting research

00:09:32.405 --> 00:09:34.824
with a team at UC Berkeley 

00:09:34.824 --> 00:09:36.409
Prof. Chenming Hu,

00:09:36.409 --> 00:09:38.035
a graduate of NTU’s Engineering dept.

00:09:38.035 --> 00:09:39.912
was teaching at UC Berkeley

00:09:39.912 --> 00:09:41.414
and happened to be a member of the team

00:09:41.414 --> 00:09:43.416
According to Hisamoto’s memoir

00:09:43.416 --> 00:09:44.625
the name FinFET

00:09:44.625 --> 00:09:46.752
was Prof. Hu’s idea

00:09:46.752 --> 00:09:48.546
Prof. Hu’s research team

00:09:48.546 --> 00:09:50.131
introduced the concept in 1998

00:09:50.131 --> 00:09:51.465
with a 17-nanometer FinFET

00:09:51.465 --> 00:09:54.302
successfully overcoming the limitations of chip miniaturization

00:09:54.302 --> 00:09:55.553
FinFET

00:09:55.553 --> 00:09:57.680
can shut the gate tightly

00:09:57.680 --> 00:09:59.599
even in extremely short channels

00:09:59.599 --> 00:10:02.435
A shorter channel means a faster switching speed

00:10:02.435 --> 00:10:03.311
and the voltage needed

00:10:03.352 --> 00:10:04.937
to operate the gate

00:10:04.937 --> 00:10:05.896
is lower

00:10:05.896 --> 00:10:07.189
It saves more power

00:10:07.189 --> 00:10:08.441
Furthermore

00:10:08.441 --> 00:10:10.151
the 3D structure enabled transistors

00:10:10.192 --> 00:10:12.111
to develop vertically

00:10:12.111 --> 00:10:13.738
Designers could use the space above the chip

00:10:13.738 --> 00:10:15.406
more efficiently

00:10:15.406 --> 00:10:15.906
FinFET

00:10:15.906 --> 00:10:19.076
had advantages in both performance and area efficiency

00:10:19.076 --> 00:10:21.162
rapidly gaining traction in the global chip industry

00:10:21.162 --> 00:10:23.164
hence started the dominating decade of FinFET

00:10:24.123 --> 00:10:25.458
Since 2014

00:10:25.458 --> 00:10:27.918
most of the process under 17 nanometers

00:10:27.918 --> 00:10:30.254
have adopted the FinFET design

00:10:30.254 --> 00:10:33.132
even with 5 and 3 nanometers production

00:10:33.132 --> 00:10:34.467
in recent years

00:10:34.467 --> 00:10:35.301
TSMC

00:10:35.301 --> 00:10:38.262
is still following the same fin-based framework

00:10:38.262 --> 00:10:41.474
TSMC rapid rise during this period

00:10:41.474 --> 00:10:44.352
was partially due to its unique advantage in FinFET technology

00:10:44.393 --> 00:10:47.313
In 2001, Prof. Hu was invited

00:10:47.313 --> 00:10:49.273
by TSMC’s President Morris Chang

00:10:49.273 --> 00:10:50.149
to return to Taiwan

00:10:50.149 --> 00:10:52.193
as TSMC’s first Chief Technology Officer

00:10:52.193 --> 00:10:54.820
bringing the latest FinFET tech to Taiwan

00:10:55.613 --> 00:10:57.323
TSMC and Samsung

00:10:57.323 --> 00:10:59.533
were locked in fierce competitionin the chip war

00:11:00.076 --> 00:11:02.787
Since the first generation of Apple’s iPhone chips

00:11:02.787 --> 00:11:04.372
were all exclusively mass-produced

00:11:04.372 --> 00:11:06.040
by Samsung

00:11:06.540 --> 00:11:09.251
However, Samsung was different from TSMC

00:11:09.251 --> 00:11:10.336
They didn’t just make chips

00:11:10.336 --> 00:11:12.380
but also design chips by themselves

00:11:12.380 --> 00:11:13.714
Apple was surprised to find out

00:11:13.714 --> 00:11:17.009
Samsung released its own S5PC100 processor

00:11:17.009 --> 00:11:19.637
which used the same core as the A4 chip

00:11:19.637 --> 00:11:21.013
for the iPhone 4

00:11:21.013 --> 00:11:22.431
which Apple had authorized Samsung to produce

00:11:22.431 --> 00:11:23.849
Moreover

00:11:23.849 --> 00:11:26.185
Samsung’s Galaxy smartphones and tablets

00:11:26.185 --> 00:11:28.813
was suspected of copying iPhone and iPad

00:11:28.813 --> 00:11:30.022
It’s not acceptable

00:11:30.398 --> 00:11:32.358
Eventually Apple and Samsung broke up

00:11:32.358 --> 00:11:34.276
On April 15, 2011

00:11:34.276 --> 00:11:35.695
Apple sued Samsung

00:11:35.695 --> 00:11:37.947
for infringement litigation

00:11:37.947 --> 00:11:39.156
at the Federal district court in San Jose,California

00:11:39.573 --> 00:11:41.409
The lawsuit lasted for 7 years

00:11:41.409 --> 00:11:44.245
Samsung ultimately compensated Apple $539 million

00:11:44.286 --> 00:11:45.079
and the case endedin a settlement

00:11:45.746 --> 00:11:48.040
Surely TSMC didn’t miss the opportunity

00:11:48.040 --> 00:11:48.999
In the beginning Morris Chang decided

00:11:48.999 --> 00:11:50.960
TSMC would only do ODM without IC design

00:11:51.001 --> 00:11:53.713
The strategy has paid off

00:11:53.713 --> 00:11:56.465
Since TSMC doesn’t produce chips under its own brand

00:11:56.465 --> 00:11:57.758
Nobody worried about

00:11:57.758 --> 00:11:59.802
TSMC plagiarizing their IC designs

00:12:00.344 --> 00:12:01.011
Besides

00:12:01.011 --> 00:12:02.972
the yield of TSMC’s 20 nanometers process

00:12:02.972 --> 00:12:04.724
was best of the world

00:12:04.724 --> 00:12:06.851
When Apple released iPhone 6

00:12:06.851 --> 00:12:08.811
all orders for A8 chips

00:12:08.811 --> 00:12:10.396
went to TSMC

00:12:10.396 --> 00:12:11.689
You think this means

00:12:11.689 --> 00:12:14.275
TSMC has won a total victory?

00:12:14.275 --> 00:12:14.900
Nope

00:12:14.900 --> 00:12:16.193
the chips a generation later

00:12:16.193 --> 00:12:18.237
suddenly reversed the game

00:12:18.237 --> 00:12:19.989
Prof. Hu’s student Liang Mong-Song

00:12:19.989 --> 00:12:21.449
defected to Samsung

00:12:21.449 --> 00:12:23.868
He helped Samsung surpass TSMC for half a year

00:12:23.909 --> 00:12:25.286
by launching the FinFET process

00:12:25.286 --> 00:12:28.080
at the 14-nanometer node, smaller than 20 nanometers

00:12:28.581 --> 00:12:30.875
the A9 chips Apple released in 2015

00:12:30.875 --> 00:12:34.253
were co-produced by Samsung and TSMC

00:12:34.253 --> 00:12:36.630
It’s the ultimate showdown

00:12:36.630 --> 00:12:38.716
Now, a decade after the duel

00:12:38.716 --> 00:12:41.260
The era of FinFET is coming to an end

00:12:41.761 --> 00:12:44.597
Major players like TSMC, Samsung and Intel

00:12:44.597 --> 00:12:45.931
are all preparing for

00:12:45.931 --> 00:12:47.433
the post-FinFET era

00:12:47.433 --> 00:12:50.186
A whole new battlefield for chips has emerged

00:12:50.728 --> 00:12:53.105
What will next-gen transistors look like?

00:12:53.105 --> 00:12:55.608
How do TSMC stay ahead?

00:12:55.608 --> 00:12:57.276
Please stay tuned to our channel

00:12:57.318 --> 00:12:59.779
See you next time

